in this video we're going to talk about  the ratio of similar figures okay so  let's go through some examples and we'll  do a problem as you would like to see it  on the act so first of all when you're  talking about the ratio of similar  figures what exactly does that mean well  first of all similar figures means that  they're the same  shape okay but not necessarily the same  size so there's a scale factor that  relates  you know how much larger how much  smaller you know one figure is in  relation to the other figure and the way  that you can find that scale factor is  let's say you were going from figure a  to figure b  what you would want to do to find that  scale factor is you take the new length  divided by the old length so i'm going  from a to b you can see the length is  five the old length was three so the  scale factor is going to be five thirds  now if i was going the other direction  from b to a then the scale factor would  be three-fifths you know you're on the  right track because if the scale factor  is greater than one it should be getting  larger right if the scale factor is  between zero and one like a three-fifths  for example that's less than one it's  getting smaller  okay now if you wanted to compare let's  say the ratio of the perimeter say you  were just looking at one of these faces  here and you wanted to compare like  what's the ratio of the perimeter of  this face to the perimeter of this face  what you would do is you would look at  the scale factor  perimeter is the same thing as length if  you were to stretch out you know  perimeter it's really just length just  like the scale factor is comparing these  two lengths so the ratio of the  perimeters would be the same as the  scale factor five thirds  but if you're comparing the area of this  face to the area of this face  area is two dimensional it's length  times width or base times height so what  you're going to do is you're going to  take that scale factor in this case 5 3  and you're going to square it so that's  25 9 you want to square the numerator  and the denominator and if you're doing  volume volume is length times width  times height so that's three dimensional  you want to take that scale factor five  thirds and you want to cube it so now  the ratio of the volumes would be 125  over 27. so let's just do an example  here to show you how this works say you  have these two  pentagonal prisms here and the volume of  this little one is 10 cubic units and we  want to find the volume of this larger  one and they tell us that the scale  factor is times two okay so everything's  being doubled  so what we would do then is we'd say  well if it's volume it's going to  actually be 2 cubed the scale factor  cube it's going to actually be 8 times  larger so 10 times 8 this volume is  actually going to be 80 cubic units okay  some students will make the mistake of  just saying oh it's 20 it's double but  it's actually the length is double the  width is double the height is doubled so  it's two times two times two it's  actually eight times larger and there  you go so let's go through this example  here  try this on your own and we can go  through it together so first of all  if a nine inch diameter personal pizza  costs eight dollars  and an extra large pizza is 18 inch  diameter if the price is proportional to  the area the pizza covers okay so when  it's laying on the cardboard there right  how much should the extra large pizza  cost okay assuming that it's  proportional to the area  okay so what you would do here is you  let's just draw a picture that always  helps so if it's a diameter is 9 that  means the radius is 4.5 and if this  larger extra large pizza has a diameter  of 18 that means its radius is 9. so you  can see the scale factor is  one to two or you can say this one is  two times larger right so if it's two  times larger the area okay is going to  be the scale factor squared it's going  to be two squared so this is actually  going to cover four times  the area like you could actually take  and put  four of these personal size pizzas  okay  inside of this larger pizza some of  these overlapping regions we could fill  in these little gaps here right  okay so let's see so basically if it's  eight dollars for the small one times  four since it's proportional it should  be thirty two dollars for the extra  large that would be your answer now in  real life they usually give you a  discount when you buy in bulk so it  might only be like twenty dollars or  something like that but uh that's how  you would do this type of problem so  review the ratio  of the scale factor the perimeters the  areas and the volumes and just be  familiar with that for the ect i'll see  in the next lesson
 

